 > Error `statusText` should be short (max 512 to 1024 characters) and only include tab, spaces or visible ASCII characters and extended characters (byte value 128–255). Prefer `message` in JSON body for extended message.
 
+## Problem Details (RFC 9457)
+
+To respond with [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html) problem details, create the error with `problem()` instead of `HTTPError`:
+
+```js
+import { problem } from "h3";
+
+app.get("/users/42", () => {
+  throw problem({
+    status: 404,
+    statusText: "Not Found",
+    message: "User not found",
+    type: "https://example.com/errors/user-not-found",
+    instance: "/users/42",
+  });
+});
+```
+
+The response uses `Content-Type: application/problem+json` and maps the fields as follows:
+
+| `problem()` input | RFC 9457 member | Notes                        |
+| ----------------- | --------------- | ---------------------------- |
+| `status`          | `status`        | Sanitized like `HTTPError`.  |
+| `statusText`      | `title`         | Omitted when not set.        |
+| `message`         | `detail`        | Omitted when not set.        |
+| `type`            | `type`          | Defaults to `"about:blank"`. |
+| `instance`        | `instance`      | Omitted when not set.        |
+| `data`            | `data`          | Kept as an extension member. |
+
+`problem()` accepts the same fields as `HTTPError`. Keys from `body` never override the RFC members. With `unhandled: true`, `detail`, `data` and `body` are hidden, as with `HTTPError`.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Errors created with `HTTPError` keep `Content-Type: application/json` and their current body. Only errors created with `problem()` use problem details.
+
 ## Unhandled Errors
 
 Any error that occurs during calling [request lifecycle](/guide/basics/lifecycle) without using `HTTPError` will be processed as an _unhandled_ error.
